course: biology
lesson: 232
title: Hidden senses: magnetic birds and turtles, ultraviolet bees, heat-seeing snakes, whiskers and lateral lines
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: We have five well-known senses, but many animals have extra ones we can only imagine: they feel the Earth's magnetism, see colours we cannot see, or feel warmth from far away.
explain: A homing pigeon let out a hundred kilometres from its loft flies back as if it had a map and compass inside its head, and in a way it has.
explain: A bee looking at a plain yellow flower sees a bright pattern pointing to the nectar, like the lit-up arrows that guide a plane onto a runway at night.
explain: Each hidden sense fits a life: a snake hunting mice in the dark feels their heat, a fish in a muddy river feels the water move, and a mole in black soil touches everything with its nose.
explain: Scientists find these senses by careful tests, for example by switching on a weak radio noise and watching robins lose their way.
fact: Homing pigeons find their way back over hundreds of kilometres using the sun, landmarks, smells and probably a sense of the Earth's magnetic field.
fact: Many migrating birds, like European robins, have an inner magnetic compass; scientists think special molecules in their eyes are involved, though how it works is still being studied.
fact: Loggerhead turtles cross whole oceans and come back years later to near the beach where they hatched, probably by remembering its magnetic «signature».
fact: Bright beach lights confuse turtle hatchlings, which crawl towards the lights instead of the moonlit sea.
fact: Bees see ultraviolet light, and many flowers have ultraviolet patterns that guide them to the nectar; bees see red poorly, so many bright red flowers are pollinated by birds instead.
fact: Rattlesnakes and other pit vipers have two heat-sensing pits on their faces, so they can find a warm mouse in total darkness.
fact: A dog's nose has hundreds of millions of smell cells, and a lamp-post tells it which dogs passed and how recently.
fact: Whiskers are deep touch hairs in pockets full of nerves; they feel tiny air movements and help a cat judge gaps, so they must never be cut.
fact: A cat's eyes seem to glow in the dark because a shiny layer at the back of the eye reflects light that comes in; no eye makes its own light.
fact: Mantis shrimps have up to 16 kinds of colour cells compared with our 3, yet tests found they are not better than us at telling close colours apart.
fact: Fish have a lateral line, a row of pores along each side that feels water movement, so they can hold their place in a current or a shoal; blind cave fish steer by it alone.
fact: Elephants hear very low rumbles we cannot hear and feel them through their feet, and they may sense distant thunderstorms.
fact: The star-nosed mole has 22 fleshy fingers on its nose and can find and eat a small worm in less than a quarter of a second.
exercise: What helps a homing pigeon find its way home? | The sun, landmarks, smells and probably a magnetic sense | Only the road signposts along the main roads and the motorways | A tiny map it carries in its beak | Following cars going the same way
exercise: How do robins seem to know north from south when they migrate? | They have an inner compass that feels the Earth's magnetic field | They follow the smell of Africa | They ask the older birds the way every single morning before they set off | They always fly towards the warmest wind
exercise: How does a sea turtle find its home beach after 20 years? | It seems to remember the beach's magnetic signature | It follows a trail of shells it left behind as a baby | Its mother waits there and calls | It can see the beach from the middle of the ocean
exercise: Why do bright beach lights harm baby turtles? | They crawl towards the lights instead of the sea | The lights burn their soft new shells | The lights make them fall asleep on the sand until the gulls come | The lights scare away their parents
exercise: Why might a bee rush to a flower that looks plain yellow to you? | It sees an ultraviolet pattern pointing to the nectar | It is attracted by the music the flower plays | Bees only like flowers with no pattern on them at all | It is colour-blind and lands anywhere
exercise: Which colour can bees hardly see? | Red | Blue | Yellow | Ultraviolet
exercise: How does a rattlesnake find a mouse in the dark? | Heat-sensing pits on its face feel the mouse's warmth | It hears the mouse's heartbeat through its forked tongue | It sees perfectly in the dark like an owl | It waits until morning
exercise: Why does a dog sniff a lamp-post for so long? | Smells tell it which dogs passed and how recently | It is looking for lost food that someone dropped near the post | It is cleaning its nose | It wants to climb it
exercise: What do a cat's whiskers tell it? | How wide a gap is and how the air moves around things | What its food tastes like before it takes a bite | Which way is north | How old the other cats in the street are
exercise: What would happen if someone cut a cat's whiskers? | It could become clumsy and confused | It would grow longer, thicker fur instead | It would see much better in the dark | Nothing at all, whiskers are only decoration
exercise: How many kinds of colour cells can a mantis shrimp have, compared with our three? | Up to 16 | Only 1 kind | Exactly 3, like us | About 1,000
exercise: What does a fish's lateral line feel? | Movement and pressure in the water | The taste of the food floating nearby | Sunlight shining on the water's surface | The beating of the fish's own heart
exercise: How do blind cave fish find their way? | Using their lateral line | By following the smell of the sun | By touching the walls with their fins only | They never move at all in the dark
exercise: How may elephants know that rain is coming far away? | They hear very low rumbles of thunder too deep for us | They read the clouds like a map | Their trunks taste rain in the air from 1,000 kilometres | Birds tell them
exercise: What does the star-nosed mole use its 22 nose fingers for? | Touching and finding food very fast in dark soil | Digging like a spade | Seeing colours | Smelling flowers from far away
test: «my mate says pigeons just follow motorways home» What is the best answer? | They may use landmarks, but also the sun, smells and probably magnetism | Right, pigeons can only follow motorways | Wrong, pigeons only ever follow the moon and the stars, never the roads | Right, pigeons get lost without roads
test: In an experiment a weak radio noise is switched on near robins. What happens? | They lose their compass direction | They sing much louder than they usually do | They fly straight to Africa at once | Nothing, birds cannot sense anything like that
test: A town near a turtle beach wants to light the beach brightly all night for tourists. What is the problem? | Hatchlings would crawl towards the lights instead of the sea | Turtles would lay more eggs | The sand would get too hot | Turtles like bright light, so there would be no problem at all for them
test: A garden is full of red flowers. Which visitors are most likely pollinating them? | Birds, because bees see red poorly | Only bees, since red is their favourite | Nobody, red flowers need no pollen moved | Moles
test: «dark tank at the zoo, the snake still hit the warm toy, how?? it's pitch black» What is right? | Pits on its face feel heat, so it «sees» warm things | Snakes have little torches in their eyes | It guessed by luck | It heard the toy breathing
test: «shut up and walk, it's just a stinky post» What is the dog really doing? | Reading smells that say which dogs passed and how recently | Trying to eat the post | Smelling for rain | Checking whether the post is warm enough for it to lie down next to
test: «mate says mantis shrimp sees way better colours than us, like 5 times better» What is right? | It has many more colour cells, but tests show it is not better at telling close colours apart | Right, it sees every colour 5 times better | Wrong, it sees only black and white | Right, and it can see through walls
test: What would happen if a fish lost its lateral line in a fast river? | It would find it harder to hold its place and feel danger in the water | It would swim faster | It could breathe air | Nothing, it is only a stripe of colour
test: Count them: 2 star-nosed moles. How many nose fingers in total? | 44 | 22 | 11 | 88
test: Count them: 3 cats and 1 dog sit by the door. How many tails and how many ears? | 4 tails and 8 ears | 3 tails and 6 ears | 4 tails and 4 ears | 8 tails and 8 ears
test: A cartoon cat hunts at night with glowing eyes that light up the room like torches. What is true? | Real eyes do not make light; they reflect light that comes in | Cats' eyes really do shine out like lamps | Only black cats have torch eyes | Cat eyes glow in the dark because they are so hot
exam: Identify the means by which pit vipers detect warm-blooded prey in conditions of total darkness. | Heat-sensitive pits situated upon the face | Echolocation by means of clicks | Detection of prey by sight alone | Electric receptors situated upon the forked tongue
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the navigation of the loggerhead turtle. | It appears to recall the magnetic characteristics of its natal beach | It navigates solely by the position of the Moon | It is guided by its mother throughout the voyage | It follows a trail of floating vegetation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the vision of the honeybee? | It perceives ultraviolet light but perceives red poorly | It perceives red light with exceptional acuity | It perceives no colour whatsoever | It perceives infrared radiation emitted by the petals of flowers
exam: State the function of the lateral line of fishes. | The detection of movement and pressure in the surrounding water | The digestion of food | The absorption of oxygen from the air | The production of colour for display
